Consider a magnetized, weakly collisional plasma described by the Vlasov–Maxwell–Landau system, in which the distribution function f_s(x,v,t) of each species s evolves under self-consistent electromagnetic fields with a small collisional dissipation. In such systems, the relevant invariant of the collisionless dynamics is the (generalized) free energy

  W = ∑_s T_s ∫ ⟨δf_s²⟩/(2 f_{0s}) d³x d³v + ∫ (|δE|² + |δB|²)/(8π) d³x,

which, in the presence of turbulence, is transported nonlinearly through both configuration space (across spatial scales k_⊥, k_∥) and velocity space (toward fine structure in v, characterized by Hermite/Laguerre wavenumber p). At sub-ion scales, several collisionless processes may operate simultaneously and compete: linear and nonlinear Landau damping, cyclotron damping, stochastic ion heating, magnetic reconnection, kinetic Alfvén-wave cascades, whistler cascades, and entropy/phase-mixing cascades.

Provide a quantitative theory of the phase-space cascade of free energy in a 3D-3V driven, weakly collisional, magnetized turbulence that:
(i) specifies how the free-energy flux is partitioned among the competing collisionless channels at ion and electron scales;
(ii) predicts the joint spectrum of free energy E(k_⊥, k_∥, p) in the inertial range between ion and electron gyroscales, including the relative weights of perpendicular spatial mixing and velocity-space (Hermite) phase mixing;
(iii) determines the species-resolved heating rates Q_i and Q_e produced by the cascade as functions of plasma parameters (β_i, T_i/T_e, k_⊥ρ_i);
(iv) characterizes the conditions under which nonlinear advection suppresses ("fluidizes") linear phase mixing versus those under which a genuine velocity-space cascade dominates.

